1
00:00:01,784 --> 00:00:03,699
[panting]

2
00:00:08,921 --> 00:00:10,445
[gate clangs]

3
00:00:16,581 --> 00:00:18,975
[continues panting]

4
00:00:19,062 --> 00:00:20,324
[creaks]

5
00:00:37,037 --> 00:00:39,648
[continues panting]

6
00:00:39,735 --> 00:00:40,692
[rattles]

7
00:00:52,530 --> 00:00:54,402
[breathing rapidly]

8
00:00:57,883 --> 00:00:59,059
[gunshot]

9
00:01:19,035 --> 00:01:20,950
[police radio chatter]

10
00:01:22,821 --> 00:01:25,955
Victim's Noah Valiquette.
32. Lives a few blocks away.

11
00:01:26,042 --> 00:01:28,523
Local PD says
a clown shot him in the back.

12
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:31,308
- Cut off his finger.
- A clown? Which finger?

13
00:01:31,395 --> 00:01:33,005
- Right index.
- Any witnesses?

14
00:01:33,093 --> 00:01:34,833
Yeah, the, uh, convenience
store owner heard the shot

15
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:36,835
caught a glimpse
of the suspect taking off.

16
00:01:36,922 --> 00:01:38,141
Now I'm thinking
that shooter forced him

17
00:01:38,228 --> 00:01:39,621
into the alley,
killed him in there.

18
00:01:39,708 --> 00:01:40,926
Did the killer take
the finger with him?

19
00:01:41,013 --> 00:01:41,971
[cell phone rings]
Looks that way.

20
00:01:44,234 --> 00:01:47,107
Uses for
a detached index finger.

21
00:01:47,194 --> 00:01:49,805
- Sending a message?
- Hmm.

22
00:01:49,892 --> 00:01:51,328
You from CBI?

23
00:01:51,415 --> 00:01:53,156
Agent Teresa Lisbon.
This is Patrick Jane.

24
00:01:53,243 --> 00:01:55,332
Ellis Hindon.
Glad your office is close.

25
00:01:55,419 --> 00:01:57,117
This is
a little out of our league.

26
00:01:57,204 --> 00:01:59,249
We tried not to screw it up.

27
00:01:59,336 --> 00:02:02,339
Preserved the crime scene,
canvassed the area for witnesses

28
00:02:02,426 --> 00:02:04,385
and put out a description
of the attacker.

29
00:02:04,472 --> 00:02:07,692
It sounds like you guys know
what you're doing. Why call us?

30
00:02:07,779 --> 00:02:09,738
This is who we picked up
off the description.

31
00:02:11,957 --> 00:02:13,089
[car door closes]

32
00:02:17,267 --> 00:02:19,139
[indistinct conversations]

33
00:02:25,014 --> 00:02:26,885
They're all crying
on the inside.

34
00:02:26,972 --> 00:02:28,887
[theme music]

35
00:02:38,984 --> 00:02:40,682
[Grace]
'You show up here
in that outfit, why?'

36
00:02:40,769 --> 00:02:41,900
[male #1]
'There was
this casting notice online.'

37
00:02:41,987 --> 00:02:43,293
Said they were holding
auditions today

38
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:45,904
for a circus movie shooting
in Solano County.

39
00:02:45,991 --> 00:02:47,210
They were looking
for clown talent

40
00:02:47,297 --> 00:02:49,125
specified we should come
in costume.

41
00:02:49,212 --> 00:02:51,432
Was there a name on the ad?
Contact information?

42
00:02:51,519 --> 00:02:54,261
No, just the address and time.
What's your name again?

43
00:02:54,348 --> 00:02:55,305
Van Pelt.

44
00:02:55,392 --> 00:02:57,742
Well, miss Van P..

45
00:02:57,829 --> 00:02:59,135
...what's this?

46
00:02:59,222 --> 00:03:02,356
You've got my heart
in your pocket.

47
00:03:02,443 --> 00:03:04,053
[imitating heartbeat]

48
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:06,490
Thanks for your time.

49
00:03:06,577 --> 00:03:08,405
- I hate clowns.
- Get in line.

50
00:03:08,492 --> 00:03:10,015
[sighs]
Same story?

51
00:03:10,102 --> 00:03:11,452
They all said they were here
for an audition.

52
00:03:11,539 --> 00:03:13,410
Keep up the interviews.
Get a solid ID

53
00:03:13,497 --> 00:03:14,585
on every one of these guys.

54
00:03:14,672 --> 00:03:15,978
Then go back
to the office

55
00:03:16,065 --> 00:03:17,980
and see if you can trace
whoever placed the ad.

56
00:03:18,067 --> 00:03:20,504
Okay. You. Come here.

57
00:03:22,332 --> 00:03:25,030
[indistinct conversations]

58
00:03:25,117 --> 00:03:27,207
- Where have you been?
- Farmers' market.

59
00:03:27,294 --> 00:03:29,992
Delicious apples, if you like
that kind of thing.

60
00:03:30,079 --> 00:03:30,993
Keeping the clowns, are you?

61
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:32,124
If the shooter's here

62
00:03:32,212 --> 00:03:33,256
I don't want him slipping away.

63
00:03:33,343 --> 00:03:35,215
Too late. He's slipped.

64
00:03:35,302 --> 00:03:36,564
These ones are
just a smoke screen.

65
00:03:36,651 --> 00:03:38,348
I mean, why else
would he place the ad?

66
00:03:38,435 --> 00:03:39,567
But it's a lovely day,
if you want to waste

67
00:03:39,654 --> 00:03:41,046
your time
talking to clowns.

68
00:03:41,133 --> 00:03:43,614
Uh, we have to.
Maybe Bozo made a mistake

69
00:03:43,701 --> 00:03:45,137
and got caught here
with the rest of 'em.

70
00:03:45,225 --> 00:03:47,401
You know,
I knew a clown once.

71
00:03:47,488 --> 00:03:50,491
Catastropho was his name.
A card thrower.

72
00:03:50,578 --> 00:03:53,320
He could stick the ace of clubs
into a melon at 50 feet.

73
00:03:53,407 --> 00:03:54,930
We need to talk
to the victim's wife.

74
00:03:55,017 --> 00:03:57,454
50 feet. Pshoo! Wow.

75
00:03:59,456 --> 00:04:02,154
If the zeta-function
is defined

76
00:04:02,242 --> 00:04:04,287
for all complex numbers

77
00:04:04,374 --> 00:04:07,247
'where "S" does not equal 1,
then--'

78
00:04:07,334 --> 00:04:08,509
[woman]
'To your audience, honey.'

79
00:04:08,596 --> 00:04:10,162
'Look at me. Talk to me.'

80
00:04:13,035 --> 00:04:14,863
I know you're just trying
to help, Daph.

81
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:16,343
'Tell me more
about the zeta-whatever.'

82
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:17,561
'You can do it.'

83
00:04:17,648 --> 00:04:19,041
I'm just not cut out
for this.

84
00:04:19,128 --> 00:04:23,741
- 'You're doing fine.'
- 'I'm sorry.'

85
00:04:23,828 --> 00:04:25,874
We made this
about two years ago

86
00:04:25,961 --> 00:04:27,615
just before we got married.

87
00:04:27,702 --> 00:04:29,834
Noah was trying to get
a tenured teaching position.

88
00:04:29,921 --> 00:04:31,575
How'd that work out for him?

89
00:04:31,662 --> 00:04:35,100
Not so good. He has trouble
addressing crowds.

90
00:04:35,187 --> 00:04:38,713
My brother was, uh,
a mathematical genius.

91
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:41,933
You know, he finished
high school at 15

92
00:04:42,020 --> 00:04:43,935
got his first degree
in, like..

93
00:04:44,022 --> 00:04:46,373
[chuckles]
...two years.

94
00:04:46,460 --> 00:04:48,592
But he wasn't good
with people.

95
00:04:48,679 --> 00:04:50,464
He was good with me.

96
00:04:50,551 --> 00:04:56,078
"Selected issues in the fourth
order differential equations."

97
00:04:56,165 --> 00:04:58,123
[chuckles]
'I guess that was Noah's.'

98
00:04:58,210 --> 00:04:59,255
Yeah.

99
00:04:59,342 --> 00:05:01,779
And, uh,
"Love's tender passion."

100
00:05:01,866 --> 00:05:02,911
Yours?

101
00:05:04,652 --> 00:05:05,566
Go ahead and make fun.

102
00:05:05,653 --> 00:05:07,002
Oh, I wouldn't dream of it.

103
00:05:07,089 --> 00:05:09,874
No, it's okay.
I didn't...I get it.

104
00:05:09,961 --> 00:05:11,789
I know how it looks.

105
00:05:11,876 --> 00:05:14,401
I'm no brainiac,
and if you met him

106
00:05:14,488 --> 00:05:16,881
you'd probably think
Noah was king of the geeks.

107
00:05:16,968 --> 00:05:18,143
What can I say?
I always had a thing

108
00:05:18,230 --> 00:05:20,407
for the smart guys.

109
00:05:20,494 --> 00:05:23,671
My girlfriends thought
I was crazy.

110
00:05:23,758 --> 00:05:25,412
'But when he opened up'

111
00:05:25,499 --> 00:05:28,240
Noah had a beautiful soul.

112
00:05:28,328 --> 00:05:31,200
And I was good for him.
I helped him with the world.

113
00:05:31,287 --> 00:05:33,071
What did he do
for a living?

114
00:05:33,158 --> 00:05:35,639
Oh, he, uh, published
a few papers

115
00:05:35,726 --> 00:05:38,294
in mathematical journals

116
00:05:38,381 --> 00:05:39,730
which, uh..

117
00:05:39,817 --> 00:05:41,689
Well, obviously,
do not pay much.

118
00:05:41,776 --> 00:05:43,778
But he was gonna do fine.

119
00:05:43,865 --> 00:05:48,435
I mean,
he was always discovering..

120
00:05:48,522 --> 00:05:50,567
What do you call 'em?
Proofs.

121
00:05:50,654 --> 00:05:53,396
and, um, inventing stuff.

122
00:05:53,483 --> 00:05:54,789
But one day
it would've paid off.

123
00:05:54,876 --> 00:05:57,574
- I know it would.
- And in the meantime?

124
00:05:57,661 --> 00:06:02,579
Um, we pretty much lived
on my waitressing.

125
00:06:02,666 --> 00:06:05,103
I think that bothered Noah
because he, um

126
00:06:05,190 --> 00:06:06,757
said
he wanted to help out more.

127
00:06:06,844 --> 00:06:10,718
Uh, he started day-trading
a few months back

128
00:06:10,805 --> 00:06:13,460
but...don't think
it went so well.

129
00:06:13,547 --> 00:06:15,679
- 'Why is that?'
- 'I don't know.'

130
00:06:15,766 --> 00:06:17,377
'He wouldn't talk about it.'

131
00:06:17,464 --> 00:06:20,031
Daphne,
he never talked about anything.

132
00:06:20,118 --> 00:06:22,294
Well, where was he going
this morning?

133
00:06:22,382 --> 00:06:26,255
Oh, um, a coffee place
downtown. Ariel's.

134
00:06:26,342 --> 00:06:30,259
Um, he spent most days
there on his laptop

135
00:06:30,346 --> 00:06:32,566
'Um, doing
his day-trading stuff.'

136
00:06:32,653 --> 00:06:34,394
He said he liked
the background frequency.

137
00:06:34,481 --> 00:06:37,440
What's going on with you?
I mean, you couldn't sit down.

138
00:06:37,527 --> 00:06:39,094
You can't stand still.
Guilty conscience?

139
00:06:39,181 --> 00:06:43,011
No. I..

140
00:06:43,098 --> 00:06:45,492
My little brother
just died.

141
00:06:45,579 --> 00:06:47,929
And he was terrified
of clowns

142
00:06:48,016 --> 00:06:49,539
so whoever did this
was sick.

143
00:06:49,626 --> 00:06:52,281
Did you know about
Noah's thing with clowns?

144
00:06:52,368 --> 00:06:54,631
Yeah, uh, Noah
had his quirks.

145
00:06:54,718 --> 00:06:57,373
'And for the record,
where were you?'

146
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:00,289
- 'This morning at 7:30?'
- Uh, I was here, asleep.

147
00:07:00,376 --> 00:07:01,551
I worked a late shift
last night.

148
00:07:01,638 --> 00:07:04,467
And you?

149
00:07:04,554 --> 00:07:07,339
Uh, in my car,
on the way to work.

150
00:07:07,427 --> 00:07:09,429
Tough game
Noah had going here.

151
00:07:09,516 --> 00:07:12,301
He...wasn't playing
either of you?

152
00:07:12,388 --> 00:07:14,521
- No.
- Who did he play with?

153
00:07:14,608 --> 00:07:17,219
Uh, mostly one guy.
Uh, Tolman Bunting.

154
00:07:17,306 --> 00:07:19,047
'Tolman Bunting.
He runs a store'

155
00:07:19,134 --> 00:07:20,309
called "The Puzzle Nook"
not too far

156
00:07:20,396 --> 00:07:21,571
from the Valiquettes'.

157
00:07:21,658 --> 00:07:22,833
Phone records show
a lot of calls

158
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:24,487
between the store
and the house.

159
00:07:24,574 --> 00:07:26,663
We'll go and talk to him.
How's it going with the clowns?

160
00:07:26,750 --> 00:07:28,230
'Kenmore PD found
a clown's outfit'

161
00:07:28,317 --> 00:07:29,536
'on the side of the road
about 3 miles'

162
00:07:29,623 --> 00:07:30,624
'from the crime scene.'

163
00:07:30,711 --> 00:07:32,277
No trace of DNA, though.

164
00:07:32,364 --> 00:07:33,670
Well, we'll put the clowns
aside for now then

165
00:07:33,757 --> 00:07:35,063
but keep the files nearby

166
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:36,194
in case we need
to go back to them.

167
00:07:36,281 --> 00:07:37,326
Okay, boss. You got it.

168
00:07:41,025 --> 00:07:44,420
[man]
'Noah's mind was exquisite.'

169
00:07:44,507 --> 00:07:46,944
A few years ago I published
a book of number puzzles

170
00:07:47,031 --> 00:07:50,121
and, uh, shortly after,
Noah e-mailed me.

171
00:07:50,208 --> 00:07:51,471
He'd, uh--he'd solved
all the puzzles

172
00:07:51,558 --> 00:07:52,646
of course,
but he had also found

173
00:07:52,733 --> 00:07:55,213
a few logical flaws
in two of them.

174
00:07:55,300 --> 00:07:57,477
That's when I realized
that I had to have a person

175
00:07:57,564 --> 00:08:00,480
of his intellectual caliber
in my orbit.

176
00:08:00,567 --> 00:08:02,090
Mm, soon after that

177
00:08:02,177 --> 00:08:03,657
we started playing
a regular game of chess.

178
00:08:03,744 --> 00:08:05,572
How often did you see him?

179
00:08:05,659 --> 00:08:08,836
Mm... once a month,
I'd say, but we, uh..

180
00:08:08,923 --> 00:08:12,709
We'd play on the internet
or on the phone, and..

181
00:08:12,796 --> 00:08:15,190
Uh, I think you'll, uh...
you'll like that one.

182
00:08:15,277 --> 00:08:16,626
It's rather tricky.

183
00:08:16,713 --> 00:08:19,237
Doesn't this
just kinda go like that..

184
00:08:19,324 --> 00:08:21,109
'around there...
that goes around there...'

185
00:08:21,196 --> 00:08:22,327
over there like that

186
00:08:22,414 --> 00:08:26,723
and...that's it, right?

187
00:08:26,810 --> 00:08:28,072
Very good.

188
00:08:28,159 --> 00:08:29,552
'Yes.'

189
00:08:29,639 --> 00:08:31,902
Hmm. That was fun.

190
00:08:31,989 --> 00:08:34,688
Uh, do you know anybody
who was angry with Noah

191
00:08:34,775 --> 00:08:37,342
or maybe somebody who's held
a grudge up against him?

192
00:08:37,429 --> 00:08:39,823
No, no, not at... all,
but, see

193
00:08:39,910 --> 00:08:42,652
Noah was a bit oblivious
to people's feelings

194
00:08:42,739 --> 00:08:45,481
so sometimes that would come
across as rude.

195
00:08:45,568 --> 00:08:47,222
But he's honestly the-the-the

196
00:08:47,309 --> 00:08:48,876
gentlest person
that you can imagine.

197
00:08:48,963 --> 00:08:52,749
I-I-I can't fathom
anyone wanting to kill Noah.

198
00:08:52,836 --> 00:08:54,403
Were you aware
that he was coulrophobic?

199
00:08:54,490 --> 00:08:55,535
That means
that he was afraid of--

200
00:08:55,622 --> 00:08:59,190
Fear of clowns.
No, not at all.

201
00:08:59,277 --> 00:09:01,018
- You sure?
- Quite.

202
00:09:01,105 --> 00:09:03,325
'Where were you
this morning at 7:30?'

203
00:09:03,412 --> 00:09:06,458
I was out for a run.

204
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:08,591
Was this the game that
you were playing with Noah?

205
00:09:08,678 --> 00:09:10,201
As a matter of fact,
it was.

206
00:09:10,288 --> 00:09:14,118
Hmm. Yeah, I think
he might have had you.

207
00:09:14,205 --> 00:09:17,034
- You play?
- Oh...a little.

208
00:09:17,121 --> 00:09:18,949
We should have a game
sometime.

209
00:09:19,036 --> 00:09:22,431
That works. I'll open
with pawn to c4.

210
00:09:22,518 --> 00:09:23,693
[clatter]

211
00:09:25,303 --> 00:09:27,392
- Pawn to e6.
- Knight to f3.

212
00:09:27,479 --> 00:09:28,829
Pawn to d5.

213
00:09:28,916 --> 00:09:33,094
Declining the gambit.
Interesting. Pawn to d4.

214
00:09:33,181 --> 00:09:35,357
- Oh, really?
- Scout's honor.

215
00:09:35,444 --> 00:09:37,272
Could we get back
to the interview, please?

216
00:09:37,359 --> 00:09:39,187
Of course. My apologies.

217
00:09:39,274 --> 00:09:41,319
- You're gonna regret that open.
- We'll see.

218
00:09:41,406 --> 00:09:44,279
Did you notice anything unusual
in Noah's behavior recently?

219
00:09:44,366 --> 00:09:47,151
No. There...
there was one thing.

220
00:09:47,238 --> 00:09:51,939
When we, uh, when we actually
started that game

221
00:09:52,026 --> 00:09:54,681
Noah was quite intent
on betting

222
00:09:54,768 --> 00:09:56,291
which he had never done
before.

223
00:09:56,378 --> 00:09:57,553
Did you take the bet?

224
00:09:57,640 --> 00:09:59,381
No,
I like my money too much.

225
00:09:59,468 --> 00:10:00,991
You sure you want
to stick with that open?

226
00:10:01,078 --> 00:10:02,906
Why not?

227
00:10:02,993 --> 00:10:04,908
Knight to f6.

228
00:10:04,995 --> 00:10:05,909
Knight to c3.

229
00:10:05,996 --> 00:10:09,391
Bishop...to e7.

230
00:10:09,478 --> 00:10:11,306
Hmm.
Your funeral.

231
00:10:11,393 --> 00:10:14,570
- Thank you for your time.
- Thank you.

232
00:10:14,657 --> 00:10:16,137
Thank you
for stopping by.

233
00:10:16,224 --> 00:10:20,141
By the way, bishop...g5.

234
00:10:20,228 --> 00:10:21,316
Mind your queen.

235
00:10:21,403 --> 00:10:22,839
[door creaks]

236
00:10:25,102 --> 00:10:29,063
Hey, you got a message
from somebody named Tolman.

237
00:10:29,150 --> 00:10:30,281
'"King side castle."'

238
00:10:30,368 --> 00:10:31,674
Aah.

239
00:10:31,761 --> 00:10:33,850
[clicks switch]

240
00:10:33,937 --> 00:10:36,070
As he leans
toward his doom.

241
00:10:36,157 --> 00:10:38,725
Chess, the game of kings.

242
00:10:38,812 --> 00:10:40,378
Kings with too much time
on their hands.

243
00:10:40,465 --> 00:10:42,598
[Van Pelt]
Hey, boss? What's up?

244
00:10:42,685 --> 00:10:44,426
Noah Valiquette's wife said
that he was bringing in money

245
00:10:44,513 --> 00:10:45,557
by day-trading.

246
00:10:45,645 --> 00:10:46,863
- Right?
- That's right.

247
00:10:46,950 --> 00:10:48,865
They had
a little money in the bank

248
00:10:48,952 --> 00:10:50,519
but I checked
his web browser history.

249
00:10:50,606 --> 00:10:53,478
There isn't a single trade,
so I ran his social

250
00:10:53,565 --> 00:10:54,958
but he's never even owned
a stock.

251
00:10:55,045 --> 00:10:57,221
- What was he doing then?
- I can't tell.

252
00:10:57,308 --> 00:10:58,701
There's a lot of files

253
00:10:58,788 --> 00:11:01,008
that seem like
random numbers and letters.

254
00:11:01,095 --> 00:11:03,532
Every week, he e-mailed a bunch
of them to the same address.

255
00:11:03,619 --> 00:11:06,883
- "Beastslayer 949."
- Contact Noah's ISP.

256
00:11:06,970 --> 00:11:09,277
Make them tell you
who Beastslayer is.

257
00:11:09,364 --> 00:11:11,192
Hey, boss?
Daphne Valiquette on 2.

258
00:11:11,279 --> 00:11:12,193
'She sounds freaked.'

259
00:11:15,109 --> 00:11:16,588
- Daphne?
- 'You have to come.'

260
00:11:16,676 --> 00:11:18,373
'There's somebody
inside my house.'

261
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:21,550
'I-I called
as soon as I saw the door.'

262
00:11:21,637 --> 00:11:22,943
It's alright.
Just take it easy.

263
00:11:23,030 --> 00:11:24,553
I just...I was gone
less than an hour.

264
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:25,554
I should've called
the police, I know.

265
00:11:25,641 --> 00:11:27,034
Um, I-I wasn't thinking.

266
00:11:27,121 --> 00:11:28,383
How many entrances
besides the front?

267
00:11:28,470 --> 00:11:29,819
- Uh, just the back door.
- Got it.

268
00:11:29,906 --> 00:11:31,647
- You guys take the front.
- Oh.

269
00:11:34,258 --> 00:11:35,607
Somebody forced it open.

270
00:11:46,140 --> 00:11:49,752
[Cho]
Kitchen's clear.

271
00:11:49,839 --> 00:11:50,971
[Rigsby]
Bathroom's clear!

272
00:11:51,058 --> 00:11:53,321
[Van Pelt]
Living room's clear.

273
00:11:53,408 --> 00:11:55,932
[Cho]
Bedroom's clear.

274
00:11:56,019 --> 00:11:57,194
Hey, guys,
you need to see this.

275
00:12:05,768 --> 00:12:06,943
Whoa.

276
00:12:07,030 --> 00:12:08,771
Somebody really wanted
something.

277
00:12:08,858 --> 00:12:10,773
Safe in the floor.
That's pretty old-school.

278
00:12:12,993 --> 00:12:14,211
That's
a high-end sensor lock.

279
00:12:14,298 --> 00:12:15,952
It's gonna be tough
to bust into.

280
00:12:16,039 --> 00:12:18,259
I'll call tech services,
tell 'em we need a locksmith.

281
00:12:18,346 --> 00:12:19,303
[Lisbon]
'Uh, don't bother.'

282
00:12:21,479 --> 00:12:23,438
I found the key.

283
00:12:23,525 --> 00:12:25,396
No, this type of safe
doesn't use a key.

284
00:12:25,483 --> 00:12:27,268
- Oh, yes, it does.
- Ew.

285
00:12:30,271 --> 00:12:31,794
[beeps, click]

286
00:12:34,057 --> 00:12:35,058
Bag it.

287
00:12:40,585 --> 00:12:42,022
Ew.

288
00:12:59,474 --> 00:13:01,389
You're telling me that
somebody killed my husband

289
00:13:01,476 --> 00:13:03,826
and cut off his finger
to get into some safe?

290
00:13:03,913 --> 00:13:06,350
Yes, and this is
all we found in there...

291
00:13:06,437 --> 00:13:08,570
a chessboard, a comic book
and some papers.

292
00:13:08,657 --> 00:13:10,354
Have you ever seen
this stuff before?

293
00:13:10,441 --> 00:13:11,660
No.

294
00:13:11,747 --> 00:13:13,053
Did Noah ever mention
the safe?

295
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:14,968
No. Never. We...

296
00:13:15,055 --> 00:13:17,318
This was his house
before we got married.

297
00:13:17,405 --> 00:13:20,800
I just moved my stuff in.
Maybe you can tell...

298
00:13:20,887 --> 00:13:23,411
volunteering information
wasn't Noah's strong suit.

299
00:13:23,498 --> 00:13:24,891
Well, we think whatever
the killer was looking for

300
00:13:24,978 --> 00:13:26,501
wasn't in the safe.

301
00:13:26,588 --> 00:13:28,111
That's why they ripped up
the rest of the house.

302
00:13:28,198 --> 00:13:29,721
[cell phone rings]
Would you excuse me?

303
00:13:29,809 --> 00:13:31,549
Yeah.

304
00:13:31,636 --> 00:13:32,594
Lisbon.

305
00:13:40,602 --> 00:13:42,517
[sniffles]

306
00:14:04,756 --> 00:14:05,757
[Lisbon]
'Jane!'

307
00:14:09,849 --> 00:14:11,981
[Lisbon]
'Cho got in touch
with Valiquette's ISP.'

308
00:14:12,068 --> 00:14:14,592
"Beastslayer 949" is
Alec Mosca.

309
00:14:14,679 --> 00:14:15,942
He lives 15 miles away.

310
00:14:16,029 --> 00:14:17,334
Cho and Rigsby are
on their way now.

311
00:14:17,421 --> 00:14:19,423
Splendid.

312
00:14:19,510 --> 00:14:22,252
Did Noah have
any other hiding places?

313
00:14:22,339 --> 00:14:24,602
Maybe. Yeah, sure.
I don't know.

314
00:14:24,689 --> 00:14:27,562
I mean, I feel like
I don't know anything anymore.

315
00:14:27,649 --> 00:14:28,998
I mean,
how many secrets did he keep?

316
00:14:29,085 --> 00:14:31,131
Why...why put
all this junk into a safe?

317
00:14:31,218 --> 00:14:34,525
Well, it's not all junk.
This is a "Spider-man 46".

318
00:14:34,612 --> 00:14:37,093
In this issue,
he takes on "The Shocker".

319
00:14:37,180 --> 00:14:40,357
'It's very good.
Worth a pretty penny.'

320
00:14:40,444 --> 00:14:43,491
But what I find is
interesting

321
00:14:43,578 --> 00:14:46,537
is that the chess set
that your husband has set up

322
00:14:46,624 --> 00:14:48,670
on the table is expensive

323
00:14:48,757 --> 00:14:50,498
and this one,
on the other hand

324
00:14:50,585 --> 00:14:54,458
is made in china...
not so expensive.

325
00:14:54,545 --> 00:14:57,461
So one has to wonder
why he had the cheapo set

326
00:14:57,548 --> 00:14:59,246
hidden away so carefully

327
00:14:59,333 --> 00:15:00,551
unless..

328
00:15:03,206 --> 00:15:04,120
[rattles]

329
00:15:06,644 --> 00:15:08,298
There's something inside.

330
00:15:12,607 --> 00:15:13,913
Locker key.

331
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,393
Locker 42.
I wonder where that is.

332
00:15:21,616 --> 00:15:23,444
- This the one?
- Yep.

333
00:15:23,531 --> 00:15:27,274
[knocks on door]

334
00:15:27,361 --> 00:15:30,494
- Yeah?
- Alec Mosca?

335
00:15:30,581 --> 00:15:31,495
Why? Who are you?

336
00:15:31,582 --> 00:15:33,671
Agents Cho and Rigsby, CBI.

337
00:15:33,758 --> 00:15:34,890
Please open the door.

338
00:15:34,977 --> 00:15:36,848
- Who?
- CBI, sir. Let us in.

339
00:15:36,936 --> 00:15:39,721
Uh...sure.
CBI, no problem.

340
00:15:39,808 --> 00:15:41,418
Uh, look,
I'm in my skivvies

341
00:15:41,505 --> 00:15:45,292
so, uh, give me a second
to get presentable, alright?

342
00:15:45,379 --> 00:15:47,294
Think he's gonna put his pants
on before he hits the window?

343
00:15:47,381 --> 00:15:48,686
No, I do not.

344
00:15:48,773 --> 00:15:50,253
[knocks on door]
Mr. Mosca!

345
00:15:56,390 --> 00:15:58,087
- Let me see your hands!
- Get away from the window!

346
00:15:58,174 --> 00:15:59,915
- Let me see your hands!
- Alright! Okay.

347
00:16:00,002 --> 00:16:01,656
No problem.
You see that?

348
00:16:01,743 --> 00:16:03,658
There's $14,000 in it.
Take that to Mike.

349
00:16:03,745 --> 00:16:05,094
Tell him
I'm good for the rest.

350
00:16:05,181 --> 00:16:07,009
On my sister-in-law's grave,
I swear!

351
00:16:07,096 --> 00:16:08,315
What the hell
are you talking about?

352
00:16:08,402 --> 00:16:09,969
Mike sent you, right?

353
00:16:10,056 --> 00:16:13,450
To collect
on the Boston-Philly bet?

354
00:16:13,537 --> 00:16:15,626
Oh, crap.

355
00:16:15,713 --> 00:16:18,673
Face the wall.
Put your hands on your head.

356
00:16:18,760 --> 00:16:23,460
[handcuffs rattle and click]
Who are you guys?

357
00:16:23,547 --> 00:16:26,463
- Remember the badge? CBI.
- What the hell is that?

358
00:16:26,550 --> 00:16:27,638
California
Bureau of Investigation.

359
00:16:27,725 --> 00:16:29,162
What the hell is that?

360
00:16:29,249 --> 00:16:31,077
We're like the FBI,
only more conveniently located.

361
00:16:37,344 --> 00:16:38,519
Thanks.

362
00:16:38,606 --> 00:16:41,043
Let me say I'm very sorry

363
00:16:41,130 --> 00:16:43,524
for the misunderstanding
at my door.

364
00:16:43,611 --> 00:16:45,265
I had no idea you were cops.

365
00:16:45,352 --> 00:16:47,615
We showed you our badges
and identified ourselves.

366
00:16:47,702 --> 00:16:49,834
You never watch movies?
That's how the bad guys do it.

367
00:16:49,921 --> 00:16:51,010
No, it's not. Sit down.

368
00:16:57,712 --> 00:16:59,279
How do you know
Noah Valiquette?

369
00:16:59,366 --> 00:17:01,759
Coffeehouse.
Uh, Noah likes Vienna roast.

370
00:17:01,846 --> 00:17:03,761
I'm partial
to the Sumatran blend.

371
00:17:03,848 --> 00:17:06,329
Mm-hmm. Is that what
he's e-mailing you about..

372
00:17:06,416 --> 00:17:07,417
...coffee orders?

373
00:17:09,941 --> 00:17:11,030
You should tell the truth,
Mr. Mosca.

374
00:17:11,117 --> 00:17:12,031
It's easier to remember.

375
00:17:13,684 --> 00:17:17,340
This is how it is.
I occasionally place a wager.

376
00:17:17,427 --> 00:17:19,038
Meaning you're
a degenerate gambler?

377
00:17:19,125 --> 00:17:21,475
Yeah, okay.
A couple months back

378
00:17:21,562 --> 00:17:23,433
I'm in the coffeehouse
going through my picks

379
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:26,741
when Noah asks what I'm doing.
Long story short..

380
00:17:26,828 --> 00:17:29,961
he says he can give me
"rational analysis" on the odds.

381
00:17:30,049 --> 00:17:31,702
So I threw him a couple games.

382
00:17:31,789 --> 00:17:34,140
Bing, bing, bing,
they all came through.

383
00:17:34,227 --> 00:17:36,142
That caught my eye,
so we made a deal.

384
00:17:36,229 --> 00:17:38,579
He gave me picks.
I gave him 10% of the winnings.

385
00:17:38,666 --> 00:17:40,015
These don't look like picks.

386
00:17:40,102 --> 00:17:41,973
Noah said he had
to send them in code.

387
00:17:42,061 --> 00:17:45,325
Don't ask me.
Just who he was.

388
00:17:45,412 --> 00:17:47,109
When did the arrangement
go south?

389
00:17:47,196 --> 00:17:48,545
- Who said it did?
- You did.

390
00:17:48,632 --> 00:17:50,286
You were nervous
ever since I mentioned his name.

391
00:17:50,373 --> 00:17:54,856
From three weeks back, every one
of Noah's calls is a bust.

392
00:17:54,943 --> 00:17:56,379
Guy couldn't pick a bear
to crap in the woods.

393
00:17:56,466 --> 00:17:58,120
I asked him what was going on.

394
00:17:58,207 --> 00:18:00,427
All he'd say was he had
a problem, couldn't focus.

395
00:18:00,514 --> 00:18:01,863
That must have made you angry.

396
00:18:01,950 --> 00:18:03,691
You think? He's got a problem?

397
00:18:03,778 --> 00:18:05,780
I'm the one bleeding money.

398
00:18:05,867 --> 00:18:07,216
But anything that
you might have heard

399
00:18:07,303 --> 00:18:09,871
about angry words between us

400
00:18:09,958 --> 00:18:12,003
bears no relation
to deeds carried out.

401
00:18:12,091 --> 00:18:14,136
I never touched the guy.

402
00:18:14,223 --> 00:18:16,095
Mosca's got two arrests
for assault.

403
00:18:16,182 --> 00:18:17,357
I could see him
going after Noah.

404
00:18:17,444 --> 00:18:20,273
In a bar fight, maybe.
Not in a clown suit.

405
00:18:20,360 --> 00:18:24,277
The question is,
why did Noah's picks go sour?

406
00:18:24,364 --> 00:18:27,236
- Anybody can have a bad run.
- 'But Noah wasn't anybody.'

407
00:18:27,323 --> 00:18:30,587
I mean, a day here, a day there.
But three weeks?

408
00:18:30,674 --> 00:18:32,111
According to Mosca,
he had a problem.

409
00:18:32,198 --> 00:18:34,113
He couldn't focus.
Troubles at home maybe?

410
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:36,376
That doesn't sound like the kind
of problem he'd even notice.

411
00:18:36,463 --> 00:18:38,769
It must have been some kind
of intellectual challenge.

412
00:18:38,856 --> 00:18:40,380
Agreed. But who could present

413
00:18:40,467 --> 00:18:41,729
that kind
of intellectual challenge

414
00:18:41,816 --> 00:18:44,514
that kind of...puzzle, eh?

415
00:18:45,559 --> 00:18:47,126
Oh, Mr. Jane.

416
00:18:47,213 --> 00:18:50,041
Did you, um, did you come here
to concede the game?

417
00:18:50,129 --> 00:18:52,653
Not at all.

418
00:18:52,740 --> 00:18:55,221
Bishop takes e7.

419
00:18:55,308 --> 00:18:57,310
'Interesting.'

420
00:18:57,397 --> 00:18:59,790
What problem was Noah Valiquette
working on for you?

421
00:18:59,877 --> 00:19:02,663
I don't know what you're talking
about. Noah and I played chess.

422
00:19:02,750 --> 00:19:05,622
- Liar.
- I am not a liar.

423
00:19:05,709 --> 00:19:07,798
What makes you think I can't
tell when someone is lying?

424
00:19:07,885 --> 00:19:10,801
- Jane.
- Huh? Enough, Mr. Tolman.

425
00:19:10,888 --> 00:19:12,890
Start talking the truth
or I'm gonna pop ya.

426
00:19:12,977 --> 00:19:14,109
Don't even think about it.

427
00:19:14,196 --> 00:19:15,937
Put the gun down.
Put it down.

428
00:19:17,765 --> 00:19:19,767
It's soft. It's just a hand.

429
00:19:19,854 --> 00:19:21,116
There's...
there's nothing there.

430
00:19:21,203 --> 00:19:24,075
Just a...just a hand.
We're good.

431
00:19:24,163 --> 00:19:26,165
Annika, put the gun down.

432
00:19:28,906 --> 00:19:32,519
Why do you have
an armed bodyguard, Mr. Bunting?

433
00:19:32,606 --> 00:19:35,304
Why don't we, um,
step into my office?

434
00:19:35,391 --> 00:19:36,784
Okay?

435
00:19:41,092 --> 00:19:42,311
Sweet.

436
00:19:42,398 --> 00:19:44,357
Do you understand
how important

437
00:19:44,444 --> 00:19:46,707
puzzles and codes
are to you agents?

438
00:19:46,794 --> 00:19:50,450
They protect your e-mails.
They stop credit card fraud.

439
00:19:50,537 --> 00:19:52,495
'Puzzle making and breaking'

440
00:19:52,582 --> 00:19:55,368
'is the preeminent security
issue of our day, and I run'

441
00:19:55,455 --> 00:19:59,198
a consulting firm that deals
specifically with those issues.

442
00:19:59,285 --> 00:20:01,635
- Who do you work for?
- We're not prejudiced.

443
00:20:01,722 --> 00:20:03,680
- Whoever needs us.
- What was Noah doing for you?

444
00:20:03,767 --> 00:20:05,639
That's delicate.

445
00:20:05,726 --> 00:20:09,251
Mr. Bunting, unless you
give us your full cooperation

446
00:20:09,338 --> 00:20:11,253
I'll have
the CBI computer crimes unit

447
00:20:11,340 --> 00:20:13,124
in this office in an hour.

448
00:20:15,170 --> 00:20:18,217
I was grooming Noah
for a, uh, pet project.

449
00:20:18,304 --> 00:20:20,871
'Something special.'

450
00:20:20,958 --> 00:20:21,916
What for?

451
00:20:23,918 --> 00:20:26,225
A device that could decipher

452
00:20:26,312 --> 00:20:28,531
virtually any
encrypted information.

453
00:20:28,618 --> 00:20:30,141
The universal hack.

454
00:20:30,229 --> 00:20:32,927
'Well, that's the crude way
of calling it.'

455
00:20:33,014 --> 00:20:35,059
I identified three people that
I thought could pull it off

456
00:20:35,146 --> 00:20:36,496
and Noah was one of them.

457
00:20:36,583 --> 00:20:40,239
I offered each one of them
$2 million.

458
00:20:40,326 --> 00:20:42,458
Last week,
Noah called me and told me

459
00:20:42,545 --> 00:20:45,200
that he had built the device

460
00:20:45,287 --> 00:20:47,289
but that he wasn't
going to give it to me.

461
00:20:47,376 --> 00:20:48,508
Why?

462
00:20:48,595 --> 00:20:50,553
He said he had second thoughts.

463
00:20:50,640 --> 00:20:51,728
He was worried about the device

464
00:20:51,815 --> 00:20:53,164
falling into the wrong hands.

465
00:20:53,252 --> 00:20:54,557
And that doesn't worry you?

466
00:20:54,644 --> 00:20:56,646
I'm a businessman,
not a moralist.

467
00:20:56,733 --> 00:20:58,953
Hmm. Kind of annoying,
though

468
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:00,476
Noah holding out
on you like that.

469
00:21:00,563 --> 00:21:02,826
Not really.

470
00:21:02,913 --> 00:21:05,960
You see, I decided that Noah
didn't really build the device

471
00:21:06,047 --> 00:21:08,919
that he couldn't, and he was
embarrassed by his failures.

472
00:21:09,006 --> 00:21:10,399
What if he had
built a device?

473
00:21:10,486 --> 00:21:11,835
What would you do
to get your hands on it?

474
00:21:11,922 --> 00:21:13,663
Anything..

475
00:21:13,750 --> 00:21:16,492
...short of killing him,
of course.

476
00:21:16,579 --> 00:21:18,320
'Cause I found a locker key
at Noah's house

477
00:21:18,407 --> 00:21:20,844
very well-hidden...
a locker 42.

478
00:21:20,931 --> 00:21:22,803
Interesting. Do you have
any idea where that locker is?

479
00:21:22,890 --> 00:21:23,847
We're looking.

480
00:21:23,934 --> 00:21:24,935
'Well, if you find it'

481
00:21:25,022 --> 00:21:26,807
you must let me know.

482
00:21:26,894 --> 00:21:29,462
Because that device is mine.

483
00:21:29,549 --> 00:21:31,812
Not yet.
You haven't paid for it.

484
00:21:31,899 --> 00:21:35,076
I have a handful of lawyers
looking into it.

485
00:21:35,163 --> 00:21:36,817
Oh, Mr. Jane..

486
00:21:36,904 --> 00:21:40,342
...before I forget...
queen to e7.

487
00:21:40,429 --> 00:21:43,171
- Taking your bishop.
- Hmm.

488
00:21:43,258 --> 00:21:44,912
We're gonna need to talk
to the other people

489
00:21:44,999 --> 00:21:46,261
that you're working
on this project with.

490
00:21:46,348 --> 00:21:47,871
Fine. No problem.

491
00:21:47,958 --> 00:21:49,960
There's an Alex Kromm
who lives in Amsterdam

492
00:21:50,047 --> 00:21:55,401
and then the, uh, third person
is local...a Oliver McDaniel.

493
00:21:55,488 --> 00:21:57,490
But I doubt
that he killed Noah.

494
00:21:57,577 --> 00:21:58,491
Why?

495
00:22:09,153 --> 00:22:10,111
C-41.

496
00:22:28,085 --> 00:22:30,218
Oliver Simon McDaniel.

497
00:22:30,305 --> 00:22:33,656
Your file says that
you finished college at 16

498
00:22:33,743 --> 00:22:36,877
graduate school at 19,
and you were sent here

499
00:22:36,964 --> 00:22:39,227
after nearly choking
your father to death.

500
00:22:39,314 --> 00:22:41,185
He's not my father.

501
00:22:41,272 --> 00:22:44,145
He's a cyber clone sent by
the galactic quantum lords.

502
00:22:44,232 --> 00:22:48,236
Oh, them. You know, they're
always up to something shady.

503
00:22:48,323 --> 00:22:51,457
Yes. They're trying
to drive me mad.

504
00:22:51,544 --> 00:22:54,285
But they won't succeed.

505
00:22:54,373 --> 00:22:55,896
Have you heard about Noah?

506
00:22:55,983 --> 00:22:59,378
I did. Sad.
Can you do a shiatsu massage?

507
00:22:59,465 --> 00:23:01,380
No.

508
00:23:01,467 --> 00:23:03,947
Shame. I'm tight.
So tight.

509
00:23:04,034 --> 00:23:07,124
And my skin is
just so dry and flaky.

510
00:23:07,211 --> 00:23:08,865
Uh-huh.

511
00:23:08,952 --> 00:23:11,215
'The visitor log says that
Noah was the only person'

512
00:23:11,302 --> 00:23:13,261
'who came to see you.
How did you know him?'

513
00:23:13,348 --> 00:23:16,699
Um, grad school.
I felt sorry for him.

514
00:23:16,786 --> 00:23:18,179
He was a little off.

515
00:23:18,266 --> 00:23:22,009
My guess was radiation.
How did he die?

516
00:23:22,096 --> 00:23:23,793
Gunshot wound which, uh

517
00:23:23,880 --> 00:23:25,273
suggests
it wasn't the quantum lords

518
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:26,274
that killed him.

519
00:23:26,361 --> 00:23:30,017
Duh.
They'd use a lorax beam.

520
00:23:30,104 --> 00:23:32,323
Why would they want
to kill Noah anyway? Big wuss.

521
00:23:32,411 --> 00:23:34,761
Quite. His killer
wore a clown costume.

522
00:23:34,848 --> 00:23:37,198
Any ideas who
that might have been?

523
00:23:37,285 --> 00:23:40,462
Assuming, of course, that it
is a carbon-based life-form.

524
00:23:40,549 --> 00:23:43,160
Nuh-huh,
but the clown thing's clever.

525
00:23:43,247 --> 00:23:45,641
Noah hated them.

526
00:23:45,728 --> 00:23:47,643
We understand that you were
working on a problem

527
00:23:47,730 --> 00:23:49,819
for Tolman Bunting?

528
00:23:49,906 --> 00:23:51,952
Yeah. My doctors wanted to
nix it, but I convinced them

529
00:23:52,039 --> 00:23:53,954
it was good therapy.
Don't touch that!

530
00:23:54,041 --> 00:23:55,912
[sound of surf rolling]

531
00:23:58,262 --> 00:24:00,787
[turns off]
Hmm.

532
00:24:00,874 --> 00:24:04,443
[Lisbon]
'Did you know that Noah was
working on one for him, too?'

533
00:24:04,530 --> 00:24:08,621
Which makes him
a big-ass hypocrite.

534
00:24:08,708 --> 00:24:10,187
Why is that?

535
00:24:10,274 --> 00:24:12,015
Well, back in grad school,
we got offered a gig

536
00:24:12,102 --> 00:24:14,235
writing code
for some defense contractor.

537
00:24:14,322 --> 00:24:16,977
Noah passed. Said he didn't want
to do anything that could

538
00:24:17,064 --> 00:24:20,241
harm someone...like this thing
for Tolman is any better!

539
00:24:20,328 --> 00:24:22,112
It's worse.

540
00:24:22,199 --> 00:24:24,724
You could destroy the world,
practically, if you wanted to.

541
00:24:27,117 --> 00:24:29,119
Noah may well have
completed that project.

542
00:24:29,206 --> 00:24:31,905
Yeah.
How are youdoing with it?

543
00:24:31,992 --> 00:24:33,820
Fine.

544
00:24:33,907 --> 00:24:37,824
So not even close.

545
00:24:37,911 --> 00:24:39,782
I could gouge your eyes out
with a spoon.

546
00:24:39,869 --> 00:24:41,436
I'll take a rain check
on that.

547
00:24:41,523 --> 00:24:45,788
It appears Noah may have
hidden the device in a locker

548
00:24:45,875 --> 00:24:48,225
that this key belongs to.

549
00:24:48,312 --> 00:24:51,272
Do you have any idea
where that locker could be?

550
00:24:51,359 --> 00:24:54,101
Sir, I'm a mental patient.

551
00:24:54,188 --> 00:24:56,364
I don't know anything.
Nobody does.

552
00:24:56,451 --> 00:24:59,323
But see,
I know I don't know.

553
00:24:59,410 --> 00:25:01,978
Paradox.

554
00:25:02,065 --> 00:25:03,414
Interview over.

555
00:25:03,502 --> 00:25:04,459
Yes, it is.

556
00:25:06,679 --> 00:25:08,419
Thank you.

557
00:25:08,507 --> 00:25:11,031
It's funny how well
you two get along.

558
00:25:11,118 --> 00:25:12,902
Well, I have
the same kind of trouble

559
00:25:12,989 --> 00:25:15,731
with those
pesky quantum lords.

560
00:25:15,818 --> 00:25:17,254
Noah's ethics should have
prevented him

561
00:25:17,341 --> 00:25:18,865
from taking on the project
in the first place.

562
00:25:18,952 --> 00:25:20,693
But he took it on. Why?

563
00:25:20,780 --> 00:25:23,478
Noah loved puzzles. Maybe he
couldn't resist the challenge.

564
00:25:23,565 --> 00:25:25,262
Well, there's plenty
of those to choose from.

565
00:25:25,349 --> 00:25:26,786
Thanks.

566
00:25:26,873 --> 00:25:30,006
Uh, the prospect
of lots and lots of money?

567
00:25:30,093 --> 00:25:31,442
That's a boring motive.

568
00:25:31,530 --> 00:25:32,443
Well, it's still
pretty popular.

569
00:25:32,531 --> 00:25:35,272
[alarm blaring]

570
00:25:35,359 --> 00:25:36,796
[man over PA]
'Inmate escape.'

571
00:25:36,883 --> 00:25:39,407
Open that now!

572
00:25:43,150 --> 00:25:44,412
[alarm stops]

573
00:25:44,499 --> 00:25:47,023
Oh! Alert the quantum lords.

574
00:25:47,110 --> 00:25:48,982
[imitates alarm whooping]

575
00:25:54,074 --> 00:25:55,815
'Have the Kenmore police
put out an APB.'

576
00:25:55,902 --> 00:25:58,382
And get me everything you can
on Oliver McDaniel. Thanks.

577
00:25:58,469 --> 00:26:00,994
I'm impressed.
Oliver made his own key pass

578
00:26:01,081 --> 00:26:02,822
using information stolen
from one of the guards.

579
00:26:02,909 --> 00:26:04,563
He trips the alarm
to create a distraction

580
00:26:04,650 --> 00:26:05,781
then hides
in the guards' locker room.

581
00:26:05,868 --> 00:26:07,566
The last place they'd search.

582
00:26:07,653 --> 00:26:09,568
Turns out this isn't the first
departure. Multiple exits

583
00:26:09,655 --> 00:26:11,613
unaccounted for stretching back
more than a month.

584
00:26:11,700 --> 00:26:13,310
Hospital security
didn't flag this?

585
00:26:13,397 --> 00:26:14,964
'Exits came up
under the guard's name'

586
00:26:15,051 --> 00:26:16,618
'nothing out of the ordinary.'

587
00:26:16,705 --> 00:26:18,446
So Oliver could come and go
whenever he wanted.

588
00:26:18,533 --> 00:26:19,969
Here's the kicker...he was gone
for four hours

589
00:26:20,056 --> 00:26:21,057
around the time
of Noah's murder.

590
00:26:21,144 --> 00:26:22,537
Bumps him up the suspect list.

591
00:26:22,624 --> 00:26:24,365
'There's one thing I don't get.
Why leave now?'

592
00:26:24,452 --> 00:26:26,019
Why not stick to your routine
and slip out at night?

593
00:26:26,106 --> 00:26:28,021
Because he was going
after the device.

594
00:26:28,108 --> 00:26:30,023
He must have figured out
where it was when he saw

595
00:26:30,110 --> 00:26:32,112
the locker key. Would have done
anything to get his hands on it.

596
00:26:32,199 --> 00:26:33,679
'Did you talk
to Tolman Bunting?'

597
00:26:33,766 --> 00:26:35,594
'He had no idea
where Oliver could be headed.'

598
00:26:35,681 --> 00:26:37,857
If Oliver leaves a trail,
it's not gonna be his.

599
00:26:37,944 --> 00:26:40,163
Check the guard's name
and run his credit cards.

600
00:26:40,250 --> 00:26:42,601
The security pass may not be
the only thing Oliver copied.

601
00:26:42,688 --> 00:26:43,645
Yeah, you got it.

602
00:26:46,082 --> 00:26:47,910
- Hey, Grace.
- Hey.

603
00:26:47,997 --> 00:26:50,304
Um, if I wanted to go away
by the ocean

604
00:26:50,391 --> 00:26:54,569
you know, somewhere close,
kind of a resort-type thing.

605
00:26:54,656 --> 00:26:56,266
Spa treatments?

606
00:26:56,353 --> 00:26:58,268
Yeah. Yeah, that kind of thing,
exactly.

607
00:26:58,355 --> 00:27:00,793
Oh, yeah,
that's what I'm talkin' about.

608
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:03,578
Um, is there any places
that you'd recommend?

609
00:27:03,665 --> 00:27:05,188
I'm kind of busy here, Jane.

610
00:27:05,275 --> 00:27:07,147
Well, can't you just
look it up on your computer?

611
00:27:07,234 --> 00:27:09,453
- It'll take, like, ten seconds.
- Why don't you do it yourself?

612
00:27:09,540 --> 00:27:11,368
I'll get you a nougat bar
from the, uh

613
00:27:11,455 --> 00:27:12,674
'machine over there.'

614
00:27:15,503 --> 00:27:16,896
Give me five minutes.

615
00:27:16,983 --> 00:27:18,506
You are the queen
of my heart, Grace.

616
00:27:18,593 --> 00:27:20,682
- Mm-hmm.
- I'll wait here.

617
00:27:22,684 --> 00:27:24,512
'Thank you.'

618
00:27:35,131 --> 00:27:36,742
Welcome to Tidewater, sir.

619
00:27:36,829 --> 00:27:39,396
Oh, thank you.
Very, uh, lovely place.

620
00:27:39,483 --> 00:27:42,312
We like to think so. Is there
something I can help you with?

621
00:27:42,399 --> 00:27:44,140
Uh, yes, there is.

622
00:27:44,227 --> 00:27:48,144
Um, how is your staff at
procuring, uh things for guests?

623
00:27:48,231 --> 00:27:50,756
- Um, what do you mean?
- You know, special things.

624
00:27:50,843 --> 00:27:52,148
If it's company
you're looking for--

625
00:27:52,235 --> 00:27:56,370
No, it's, uh, it's not
company I'm looking for.

626
00:27:56,457 --> 00:27:59,373
But thank you,
if you're offering company..

627
00:27:59,460 --> 00:28:00,417
...Gary.

628
00:28:02,245 --> 00:28:03,203
[typing]

629
00:28:05,161 --> 00:28:06,815
Got a hit
on the guard's credit card.

630
00:28:06,902 --> 00:28:08,251
Somebody used it to buy
a ticket to Brazil

631
00:28:08,338 --> 00:28:09,775
departing out of SFO
in an hour.

632
00:28:09,862 --> 00:28:11,124
Tell the airport police
we're on our way.

633
00:28:11,211 --> 00:28:12,125
You got it.

634
00:28:15,476 --> 00:28:16,433
[cell phone rings]

635
00:28:20,133 --> 00:28:21,874
- What, Jane?
- What's up, Lisbon?

636
00:28:21,961 --> 00:28:23,614
Oh, nothing much.
Might make a run

637
00:28:23,702 --> 00:28:26,487
to the San Francisco airport
to pick up Oliver McDaniel.

638
00:28:26,574 --> 00:28:29,098
Huh. Well, you may want
to rethink that.

639
00:28:39,979 --> 00:28:41,458
Where is he?

640
00:28:41,545 --> 00:28:45,724
Hey. Isn't this lovely?
Smell of the ocean air

641
00:28:45,811 --> 00:28:48,074
sand beneath your feet.

642
00:28:48,161 --> 00:28:50,293
You know, we should
move our offices here.

643
00:28:50,380 --> 00:28:52,426
Oliver...Jane, where is he?

644
00:28:52,513 --> 00:28:55,951
You'd be so much happier...do
wonders for your stress levels.

645
00:28:56,038 --> 00:28:57,692
He's ticketed
on a flight to Brazil.

646
00:28:57,779 --> 00:28:59,781
Cho and Rigsby are on their way
to the airport now. So..

647
00:28:59,868 --> 00:29:03,611
Oliver has been in an insane
asylum for about six months.

648
00:29:03,698 --> 00:29:06,701
He asked you for a massage.

649
00:29:06,788 --> 00:29:09,225
He's in need
of a little pampering.

650
00:29:09,312 --> 00:29:12,228
- The flight's a misdirect.
- How can you be so sure?

651
00:29:12,315 --> 00:29:14,927
The sound machine in his bedroom
was tuned in to the ocean.

652
00:29:15,014 --> 00:29:19,061
That, and the fact
that room 6-4-4

653
00:29:19,148 --> 00:29:21,150
has already ordered
six cans of SpaghettiO's

654
00:29:21,237 --> 00:29:22,630
from the concierge.

655
00:29:22,717 --> 00:29:25,067
- You can't arrest him yet.
- Yes, I can.

656
00:29:25,154 --> 00:29:27,287
No, you can't.
He escaped because he knows

657
00:29:27,374 --> 00:29:29,419
the location of the device,
and if we follow him

658
00:29:29,506 --> 00:29:30,769
he will lead us right to it.

659
00:29:30,856 --> 00:29:32,248
I don't care about the device.

660
00:29:32,335 --> 00:29:33,989
You don't care about
a code-breaking device

661
00:29:34,076 --> 00:29:35,643
that could hack
into our government's

662
00:29:35,730 --> 00:29:40,387
national security system? That's
not very patriotic, Lisbon.

663
00:29:40,474 --> 00:29:41,431
Piña colada?

664
00:29:47,916 --> 00:29:51,006
Rook to b8?

665
00:29:51,093 --> 00:29:52,791
Poor, deluded fool.

666
00:29:54,836 --> 00:29:58,448
Bunting will be resigned before
lunch, the way he's playing.

667
00:29:58,535 --> 00:30:00,320
Great.

668
00:30:02,539 --> 00:30:04,193
'Bishop..'

669
00:30:04,280 --> 00:30:06,543
...to c4.

670
00:30:09,459 --> 00:30:11,331
'There he is.'

671
00:30:11,418 --> 00:30:12,898
Cho, we have a visual.

672
00:30:12,985 --> 00:30:14,987
We'll take point.
You and Rigsby hang back.

673
00:30:16,075 --> 00:30:17,641
[indistinct conversations]

674
00:30:24,300 --> 00:30:25,824
[laughing]

675
00:30:25,911 --> 00:30:27,303
Ooh!

676
00:30:35,921 --> 00:30:38,575
He's headed in now.
Let's see where he's going.

677
00:30:40,142 --> 00:30:42,057
- Whoa!
- Whoa!

678
00:30:44,364 --> 00:30:46,148
Come on!

679
00:31:20,748 --> 00:31:22,010
Who was that?

680
00:31:22,097 --> 00:31:22,968
[breathing heavily]
I don't know.

681
00:31:23,055 --> 00:31:25,274
Watch Oliver. Cho, come on.

682
00:31:28,234 --> 00:31:29,322
Need a paramedic at the pier.

683
00:31:38,374 --> 00:31:39,332
CBI. Don't move!

684
00:32:05,184 --> 00:32:06,228
42.

685
00:32:45,528 --> 00:32:47,226
Don't move!

686
00:32:50,098 --> 00:32:51,099
'Mr. Bunting?'

687
00:32:52,405 --> 00:32:54,537
4 and 2.

688
00:32:59,412 --> 00:33:00,369
One, two.

689
00:33:05,722 --> 00:33:07,159
Yeah.

690
00:33:11,337 --> 00:33:12,947
Nice work,
tailing us to the pier.

691
00:33:13,034 --> 00:33:14,905
But I guess for a guy
who's after the universal hack

692
00:33:14,993 --> 00:33:18,257
listening in on police radios
is no big thing.

693
00:33:18,344 --> 00:33:21,042
It was, uh,
an unseasonably warm day.

694
00:33:21,129 --> 00:33:23,218
I wanted to cool off
by the water.

695
00:33:23,305 --> 00:33:25,090
It was pure coincidence
that drew me there.

696
00:33:25,177 --> 00:33:29,703
This isn't funny, Mr. Bunting.
You're in trouble.

697
00:33:29,790 --> 00:33:31,226
We've got a big handful
of felonies

698
00:33:31,313 --> 00:33:32,706
for what you did
at the pier today

699
00:33:32,793 --> 00:33:34,925
and we are this close to
nailing you for the murder

700
00:33:35,013 --> 00:33:36,144
of Noah Valiquette.

701
00:33:36,231 --> 00:33:37,276
Really?

702
00:33:37,363 --> 00:33:39,669
Really. The clown costume

703
00:33:39,756 --> 00:33:41,323
the ad...sounds like the work

704
00:33:41,410 --> 00:33:44,196
of a man who likes
to play games, doesn't it?

705
00:33:44,283 --> 00:33:47,373
Hey, boss. AG's on the phone,
says it's urgent.

706
00:33:47,460 --> 00:33:50,115
Oh, uh, do me a favor
and, uh, send my best

707
00:33:50,202 --> 00:33:51,986
to the attorney general.

708
00:33:52,073 --> 00:33:55,120
And tell Mr. Jane
queen to d8.

709
00:33:55,207 --> 00:33:58,427
Queen to d8! Thank you.

710
00:34:02,779 --> 00:34:04,172
You knew where Noah
hid the device.

711
00:34:04,259 --> 00:34:05,739
How?

712
00:34:05,826 --> 00:34:08,481
In grad school, Noah and I
would go to the pier.

713
00:34:08,568 --> 00:34:10,961
Noah liked the rides.
I liked the beach.

714
00:34:11,049 --> 00:34:14,574
All those young women
in wet bathing suits.

715
00:34:14,661 --> 00:34:16,880
I knew Noah hid the device there
as soon as I saw that key.

716
00:34:16,967 --> 00:34:19,579
So you decided to escape
and get it for yourself.

717
00:34:19,666 --> 00:34:22,799
I wanted the money. There's
an intergalactic war coming.

718
00:34:22,886 --> 00:34:24,671
- Yes, I'm aware.
- Hmm.

719
00:34:24,758 --> 00:34:26,325
But if you take
the device to Tolman

720
00:34:26,412 --> 00:34:28,109
you know we're waiting
for you in the lobby.

721
00:34:28,196 --> 00:34:31,025
Well, I wanted the device
so I could see how it worked.

722
00:34:31,112 --> 00:34:34,072
After that, I could make my own,
sell 'em wherever

723
00:34:34,159 --> 00:34:35,812
have enough money
to build my bunker.

724
00:34:35,899 --> 00:34:37,597
Now the hospital security logs
have you out

725
00:34:37,684 --> 00:34:39,773
the morning of
Noah's murder. War preparations?

726
00:34:39,860 --> 00:34:42,471
No, man. Breakfast
at my favorite strip club.

727
00:34:42,558 --> 00:34:45,561
They have an open buffet,
all you can eat.

728
00:34:45,648 --> 00:34:47,433
And Noah's killer planned
an elaborate diversion

729
00:34:47,520 --> 00:34:48,782
to get away
from the crime scene

730
00:34:48,869 --> 00:34:51,176
like your ticket
to Brazil stunt.

731
00:34:51,263 --> 00:34:54,483
I wasn't aware it was a crime
to be intelligent, Agent Cho.

732
00:34:56,703 --> 00:34:59,706
The AG insists I let
Bunting go, drop all charges.

733
00:34:59,793 --> 00:35:01,099
The man must have
serious juice.

734
00:35:01,186 --> 00:35:02,535
Well, it stands to reason.

735
00:35:02,622 --> 00:35:04,754
He knows where the skeletons
are buried.

736
00:35:04,841 --> 00:35:06,191
I don't know what to do.

737
00:35:06,278 --> 00:35:08,454
How do you build a case
against a guy like this?

738
00:35:08,541 --> 00:35:10,847
Oh...it's not so hard.

739
00:35:12,849 --> 00:35:14,590
Talk to the girls
at the strip club.

740
00:35:14,677 --> 00:35:16,679
Someone's gotta
remember I was there.

741
00:35:16,766 --> 00:35:18,681
He speaks the truth, Cho.

742
00:35:18,768 --> 00:35:22,076
Oliver here would have trouble
killing a light beer..

743
00:35:22,163 --> 00:35:23,556
...much less
Noah Valiquette.

744
00:35:23,643 --> 00:35:25,035
That's not accurate.

745
00:35:25,123 --> 00:35:26,820
I could mess you up badly
if I wanted to.

746
00:35:26,907 --> 00:35:30,432
But you don't want to. Before
we let you walk out the door

747
00:35:30,519 --> 00:35:31,825
I need a favor.

748
00:35:31,912 --> 00:35:33,566
- Not a chance.
- Consider it.

749
00:35:33,653 --> 00:35:35,742
It'll give you
the chance to work with this

750
00:35:35,829 --> 00:35:37,047
and then when you're done,
a little gift

751
00:35:37,135 --> 00:35:39,311
from the planetary league.
Hmm?

752
00:35:46,492 --> 00:35:48,233
'Rook takes f6.'

753
00:35:48,320 --> 00:35:51,410
Your very friendly assistant
let me in.

754
00:35:51,497 --> 00:35:54,326
Rook takes f6.

755
00:35:54,413 --> 00:35:58,025
King to g8.
What are you doing here?

756
00:35:58,112 --> 00:36:03,552
I came by to see if Noah's
doohickey here...actually works.

757
00:36:03,639 --> 00:36:06,076
- That's it?
- Yes.

758
00:36:06,164 --> 00:36:07,339
So he did build it.

759
00:36:07,426 --> 00:36:09,645
Yes, and I'm here
to return it..

760
00:36:09,732 --> 00:36:11,995
...for $2 million, of course.

761
00:36:12,082 --> 00:36:15,216
[chuckles]
You expect me to pay you?

762
00:36:15,303 --> 00:36:18,088
Well, not me, no,
but its rightful owners.

763
00:36:18,176 --> 00:36:21,570
Uh, have you met Noah's wife
Daphne and brother Rafe?

764
00:36:21,657 --> 00:36:25,487
Legally, Noah's property
is passed on to Daphne

765
00:36:25,574 --> 00:36:29,187
including this. Have either
of you ever seen this before?

766
00:36:29,274 --> 00:36:30,710
- No.
- What is it?

767
00:36:30,797 --> 00:36:33,843
Uh, it's a code-breaking device
designed by Noah

768
00:36:33,930 --> 00:36:37,412
for Mr. Bunting, uh,
in return for $2 million.

769
00:36:37,499 --> 00:36:39,719
- Your $2 million.
- Oh, my God.

770
00:36:39,806 --> 00:36:42,112
'I'm sure a cashier's check
will be fine, Mr. Bunting.'

771
00:36:42,200 --> 00:36:45,028
Uh, I think we first have to
make sure that this thing works.

772
00:36:45,115 --> 00:36:46,291
That was part
of our arrangement.

773
00:36:46,378 --> 00:36:47,379
As you wish.

774
00:36:52,732 --> 00:36:53,733
Hmm.

775
00:36:55,169 --> 00:36:56,257
Whoa.

776
00:36:58,825 --> 00:37:01,001
Okay, we're gonna
need an encrypted file.

777
00:37:01,088 --> 00:37:02,611
[Patrick]
'Oh, how about
Agent Lisbon's'

778
00:37:02,698 --> 00:37:03,569
'personnel file?'

779
00:37:03,656 --> 00:37:05,048
No.

780
00:37:05,135 --> 00:37:06,963
I happen to have it
on disk right here.

781
00:37:07,050 --> 00:37:08,617
- It has some great photos.
- Fine.

782
00:37:15,494 --> 00:37:17,670
[taps keys]

783
00:37:17,757 --> 00:37:19,672
[Patrick]
'It seems
to be working here.'

784
00:37:23,110 --> 00:37:24,416
[Tolman]
'Hmm.'

785
00:37:24,503 --> 00:37:25,417
What does that mean?

786
00:37:25,504 --> 00:37:29,334
It means that it's not working.

787
00:37:29,421 --> 00:37:31,901
It seems Noah was unable
to complete the project.

788
00:37:31,988 --> 00:37:33,947
I am sorry, Mrs. Valiquette.

789
00:37:34,034 --> 00:37:35,949
He needed more time.

790
00:37:36,036 --> 00:37:38,081
Wait. How do we know
it doesn't work?

791
00:37:38,168 --> 00:37:39,953
[Rafe]
'Maybe he's playing us'

792
00:37:40,040 --> 00:37:42,129
'trying to save himself
a couple million.'

793
00:37:42,216 --> 00:37:45,263
Don't be absurd. Why wouldn't
I want this to work?

794
00:37:45,350 --> 00:37:47,047
Try it again,
'cause my husband's a genius.

795
00:37:47,134 --> 00:37:48,353
'It has to work.'

796
00:37:48,440 --> 00:37:50,311
I'm afraid your,
um, husband's genius

797
00:37:50,398 --> 00:37:51,530
had its limitations.

798
00:37:51,617 --> 00:37:52,618
Humor us.

799
00:37:56,578 --> 00:37:58,493
Okay, fine.

800
00:38:07,850 --> 00:38:08,764
What's happening?

801
00:38:10,679 --> 00:38:13,291
It's working.

802
00:38:13,378 --> 00:38:14,770
It's working!

803
00:38:19,340 --> 00:38:21,429
- 'What the hell?'
- Turn it off.

804
00:38:21,516 --> 00:38:23,039
- Turn it off! Turn it off!
- Wait, wait.

805
00:38:23,126 --> 00:38:25,085
- 'It's right, isn't it?'
- Of course not.

806
00:38:25,172 --> 00:38:27,827
You said you didn't know
anything about the device.

807
00:38:27,914 --> 00:38:30,917
- You were lying.
- No. W..

808
00:38:31,004 --> 00:38:33,093
I had Oliver change the settings
so it wouldn't work.

809
00:38:33,180 --> 00:38:37,793
But you knew how to change them
back. You watched Noah work.

810
00:38:37,880 --> 00:38:40,056
You knew that he decided
not to give it to Tolman

811
00:38:40,143 --> 00:38:42,755
for any price. So you did
what you felt you had to..

812
00:38:42,842 --> 00:38:45,540
You played on his fear.
You'd take the device

813
00:38:45,627 --> 00:38:48,456
and turn it in
to Tolman yourself.

814
00:38:48,543 --> 00:38:50,371
All you had to do
was open the safe.

815
00:38:50,458 --> 00:38:52,895
The problem..

816
00:38:52,982 --> 00:38:54,375
...it wasn't in the safe.

817
00:38:59,946 --> 00:39:02,775
That movie
with the crazy math guy

818
00:39:02,862 --> 00:39:05,778
and the woman who loves him.

819
00:39:05,865 --> 00:39:08,346
Yeah, I thought Noah
and me would be like that.

820
00:39:09,912 --> 00:39:14,003
Hmm.
"Love's tender passion."

821
00:39:14,090 --> 00:39:17,398
Yeah, you go ahead
and make fun.

822
00:39:17,485 --> 00:39:19,879
Life with Noah
was nothing like that.

823
00:39:19,966 --> 00:39:22,229
It was silence...days of it

824
00:39:22,316 --> 00:39:26,015
and books I could never
understand the title of

825
00:39:26,102 --> 00:39:28,017
and no sex
when he's thinking

826
00:39:28,104 --> 00:39:31,586
which, by the way,
is all the time.

827
00:39:31,673 --> 00:39:33,501
I work 50 hours a week
scrounging for tips

828
00:39:33,588 --> 00:39:35,373
just so we could have food.

829
00:39:38,637 --> 00:39:42,292
Anyway, so then Noah
told me about this guy

830
00:39:42,380 --> 00:39:44,338
who would pay him $2 million
to build this thing..

831
00:39:44,425 --> 00:39:46,862
...$2 million.

832
00:39:46,949 --> 00:39:49,996
I begged him, and finally
he said he would do it

833
00:39:50,083 --> 00:39:52,651
which was only right
because it was his turn.

834
00:39:52,738 --> 00:39:55,654
But he wouldn't
hand it over

835
00:39:55,741 --> 00:39:58,352
and you couldn't live
with that.

836
00:39:58,439 --> 00:40:02,704
Yeah. He said
it was too dangerous.

837
00:40:02,791 --> 00:40:06,882
I tried to make him,
but he said that..

838
00:40:06,969 --> 00:40:12,366
...he said I wasn't able to
understand the implications.

839
00:40:12,453 --> 00:40:16,283
So I decided to show
him I could be smart, too.

840
00:40:16,370 --> 00:40:21,462
Or a whole lot dumber.
I'm-I'm not sure which.

841
00:40:21,549 --> 00:40:22,855
Get up.

842
00:40:22,942 --> 00:40:24,639
[handcuffs click]

843
00:40:24,726 --> 00:40:26,859
Come on.
Hands behind your back.

844
00:40:37,696 --> 00:40:38,958
What put you on to her?

845
00:40:39,045 --> 00:40:39,959
Well, I came to it very late.

846
00:40:40,046 --> 00:40:41,308
I could have kicked myself.

847
00:40:41,395 --> 00:40:43,223
The kitchen of her house
was left unsearched

848
00:40:43,310 --> 00:40:44,790
because that was her domain.

849
00:40:44,877 --> 00:40:46,444
She knew the device
wasn't there.

850
00:40:49,142 --> 00:40:51,449
'Oh, Mr. Jane.
Perfect timing.'

851
00:40:52,493 --> 00:40:55,583
Pawn to f4.

852
00:41:02,329 --> 00:41:03,286
Shrewd.

853
00:41:05,419 --> 00:41:08,770
But I gather you're here for
more than just to pin my queen.

854
00:41:08,857 --> 00:41:10,163
Yes.

855
00:41:10,250 --> 00:41:14,080
Actually, I came here
to collect my property.

856
00:41:15,951 --> 00:41:17,475
Given Daphne's condition

857
00:41:17,562 --> 00:41:19,346
ownership of the device

858
00:41:19,433 --> 00:41:21,653
'goes to Noah's brother.'

859
00:41:21,740 --> 00:41:24,569
But when I contacted Rafe,
he wanted nothing to do with it.

860
00:41:24,656 --> 00:41:26,484
So according to my attorneys

861
00:41:26,571 --> 00:41:29,443
that passes the device..

862
00:41:29,530 --> 00:41:30,488
...to me.

863
00:41:32,098 --> 00:41:33,534
I have all
the legal documents here.

864
00:41:33,621 --> 00:41:34,579
If you have any questions--

865
00:41:34,666 --> 00:41:35,841
No need. No need.

866
00:41:35,928 --> 00:41:38,713
It's all yours. All yours.

867
00:41:38,800 --> 00:41:40,149
Uh, I hope you don't mind

868
00:41:40,236 --> 00:41:41,673
I did have a little fiddle
with it.

869
00:41:41,760 --> 00:41:43,283
I just wanted
to see how it worked.

870
00:41:45,938 --> 00:41:48,767
Ohh.

871
00:41:48,854 --> 00:41:51,204
- Easily fixed, I'd imagine.
- Easily?

872
00:41:51,291 --> 00:41:53,728
Oh? Uh, what's that?

873
00:41:53,815 --> 00:41:55,121
That may be part of it.

874
00:42:01,910 --> 00:42:04,957
You are gonna be hearing
from my attorney.

875
00:42:05,044 --> 00:42:07,699
Tolman, one last thing.

876
00:42:07,786 --> 00:42:08,700
Bishop..

877
00:42:10,615 --> 00:42:12,312
...to c4.

878
00:42:14,401 --> 00:42:17,186
Forced mate in three moves.

879
00:42:19,493 --> 00:42:21,321
I win.

880
00:42:21,408 --> 00:42:23,715
'Come and take a closer look.
You'll see it.'

881
00:42:37,163 --> 00:42:39,208
We should do this again!

882
00:42:46,607 --> 00:42:47,826
[theme music]

